    justification:  # 220a
    description: "SOURCE: Process health synthesis 3.2, recommendation 8. Logging functions are the single most persistent ranking failure across all tested repos despite multiple improvement commits during the Feb 18-23 bakeoff. They have high in-degree because every module calls them, but they carry zero architectural signal. Options: (a) Name-based detection with caller-file-span threshold. (b) Behavioral detection via high in-degree + low semantic diversity. (c) Framework-aware tagging (logrus, zap, slog, log4j, winston, structlog) with infrastructure:logging category dampening. Fix should interact correctly with existing trivial-sink and utility-file dampening."  # 220a
    fields:  # 220a
      statement: "Logging functions (log, warn, error, debug, info, trace, fatal, errorf) with callers spanning many files must receive centrality dampening proportional to their ubiquity, not rank alongside architectural symbols."  # 220a
      root_cause: "Current dampening heuristics (trivial sink: out_degree<=1 AND LoC<=5, utility file detection) miss logging functions because they have moderate out-degree (calling into underlying loggers) and live in production code paths, not utility directories. XORMLogBridge.Errorf (forgejo rank 2, 389 degree), log (kong rank 2, 136 degree), Base#log (postal rank 7) all escape both heuristics."  # 220a
